    {
      "id": "sce-pmf-why-pmf-q-7c1dfd20",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Why do so many startups fail, according to the deck, and why does the deck put product-market fit first?",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "The deck begins with hard numbers: **9/10 startups fail**, and on the next slide the word startups is replaced with **products**. A further **66% drastically change their original plans**.\n\nFrom this the deck draws two points. First, the aim is **not a better Plan A but a path to a plan that works**, so you must expect to change course. Second, **Product/Market Fit is the first thing that matters**, and the Tara Hunt quote says to find it **before you run out of money**.\n\nThe rest of the deck then gives steps to get there: treat the business model as the product, brainstorm and prioritize models, spot the riskiest parts and understand the three stages of a startup.\n\nIn conclusion, product-market fit comes first because most products fail and plans change, and money is limited.",
